REGIONAL REGULATION OF SAMPANG REGENCY

NUMBER 7 OF 2017

CONCERNING

WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

REGENT OF SAMPANG,

Considering:

a. that every citizen has equal rights to live a dignified life in accordance with the principles of humanity, equality, and justice;
b. that women's empowerment is carried out so that women can actualize their potential optimally to participate in development according to their capacity;
c. that women who constitute a vulnerable group need to receive special protection so as not to experience violence and can live a decent life in accordance with the principles of humanity, equality and justice;
d. that with the enactment of Regulation of the Minister of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 6 of 2015 Concerning the System of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection, the regional government needs to improve the quality of life of women and protect children.
e. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, letter c, and letter d, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law number 12 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Regions within the Province of East Java as amended by Law Number 2 of 1965 concerning Amendment of the Territorial Boundaries of Surabaya Municipality and Surabaya Level II Region (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1965 Number 19, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 2730);
3. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
4. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
5. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
6. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 5587), as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
7. Presidential Regulation Number 87 of 2014 concerning Implementing Regulations of Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 199);
8. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Women's Protection;
9. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 3 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Child Protection;
10. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 5 of 2010 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers;
11. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 6 of 2015 concerning the System of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;
12. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 80 of 2015 concerning the Formulation of Regional Legal Products;

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF SAMPANG REGENCY

and

REGENT OF SAMPANG

DECIDES:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

The principles in Women's empowerment and child protection are:
a. Non-discrimination;
b. Best interests of the child;
c. Right to life, survival and development;
d. Respect for the views of the child;
e. Gender equality;
f. Gender justice;
g. Legal certainty.

Article 4

In order to realize the objectives as referred to in Article 3 letter a, efforts are made through:
a. providing access for women and children to education, health and other strategic services;
b. encouraging the involvement of women and children in the development process;
c. providing knowledge, skills, character values, morals and family resilience; and
d. encouraging programs that can increase women's independence in the economic, political, legal, social, cultural and other strategic fields.

Article 5

In order to realize the objectives as referred to in Article 3 letter b, efforts are made through:
a. establishment, development, and capacity strengthening of women and child protection institutions including complaint service units for violence against women and children, as well as legal aid services;
b. improving the quality of human resources of managers;
c. strengthening the institutional capacity of Gender Mainstreaming (PUG) and children;
d. strengthening and developing gender and child data systems.

Article 7

Promotive efforts as referred to in Article 6 are as follows:
a. strengthening coordination mechanisms and networks between service units in efforts to handle cases of violence;
b. providing Communication, Information, and Education (KIE) materials related to prevention and handling of violence;
c. conducting socialization, advocacy and social campaigns in the context of prevention and handling of violence.

Article 8

Preventive efforts as referred to in Article 6 are as follows:
a. conducting legal awareness education for the community, especially for women and children;
b. conducting massive and sustainable movements involving the community in actions to prevent and handle violence;
c. instilling character values, morals, and family resilience;
d. involving the role and participation of the community in women's empowerment and child protection.

Article 9

Curative efforts as referred to in Article 6 are as follows:
a. optimizing technical services related to complaints of violence against women and children;
b. providing adequate facilities and infrastructure for handling health rehabilitation, social rehabilitation, repatriation, social reintegration, and legal aid;
c. handling victims of crime and violence quickly, precisely, and accurately by law enforcement officials.

Article 12

1. The Regional Government, community, non-governmental organizations, or other institutions establish technical service units in realizing an integrated, one-stop and networked women's empowerment and child protection system;
2. The technical service unit as referred to in paragraph (1) is P2TP2A.

Article 20

Coordination can be carried out in the form of:
a. coordination meetings with work networks;
b. consultation;
c. submission of data and information; and/or
d. follow-up on case handling.
